WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.918
Picture the bakery on a busy morning.

2
00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:09.169
A few workers keep the belts moving, and none of them ever stands still.

3
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:15.191
Then someone puts a telephone at the slicer, and the old stock book has to be called about every loaf.

4
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.080
The stock book is in no hurry.

5
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.728
While the worker at the slicer waits with the phone at her ear, every loaf on her belt waits too.

6
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:28.618
In a program, the workers are threads: paths of work that each run one piece of code at a time.

7
00:00:28.618 --> 00:00:33.824
And the phone is a blocking call, a call that holds its thread until the answer comes.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.447
Reactor gives us two tools to move work between workers: publishOn and subscribeOn.

9
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:42.859
And here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:44.894
They sound like the same thing.

11
00:00:44.894 --> 00:00:46.272
Which one moves what?

12
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:50.617
First, who works each station when we say nothing at all?

13
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:55.911
The worker who puts an item on the belt carries it through every station, to the end.

14
00:00:55.911 --> 00:01:01.081
The demo ran a range, a map and a subscriber from main, and every step ran on main.

15
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:05.974
Put a timer in the chain, and the timer's own thread fires it: parallel-1.

16
00:01:05.974 --> 00:01:07.945
The map after it ran there too.

17
00:01:07.945 --> 00:01:11.825
So a chain runs on whatever thread delivered the last signal.

18
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.582
To choose the worker, you pass a scheduler: a team of threads.

19
00:01:16.582 --> 00:01:17.931
single is one worker.

20
00:01:17.931 --> 00:01:26.536
parallel has one per CPU core, the part of the processor that runs one thread at a time: a few quick hands who must never stand still.

21
00:01:26.536 --> 00:01:32.573
And boundedElastic is a back room of up to ten per core, who are allowed to wait on the phone.

22
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:34.579
Now the first tool.

23
00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:38.052
publishOn sits on the belt itself: a hand-over point.

24
00:01:38.052 --> 00:01:45.065
Items arrive from above on any thread, wait in a small queue, and a worker of the new team carries them on.

25
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.369
Put publishOn(single) between two stations.

26
00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:49.843
Which stations move?

27
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:54.346
Only the ones after it.

28
00:01:54.346 --> 00:01:56.212
The source still ran on main.

29
00:01:56.212 --> 00:01:59.557
After the hand-over point, the item was on single-1.

30
00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:02.131
Everything before it stays where it was.

31
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.821
The second tool works on the other path.

32
00:02:05.821 --> 00:02:12.601
When you subscribe, the subscription travels up the chain, from your end to the source: you sign the order form.

33
00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:16.839
subscribeOn is a line on that form, saying which team starts the oven.

34
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.532
So here's publishOn(single) in the middle, and subscribeOn(boundedElastic) written at the very end.

35
00:02:23.532 --> 00:02:25.248
Where does the source run?

36
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:31.893
On boundedElastic-1, although subscribeOn was written last.

37
00:02:31.893 --> 00:02:37.302
The form is read before the oven starts, so it doesn't matter where you write that line.

38
00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:41.236
And after the hand-over point, the item still moved to single-1.

39
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.185
What if the form has two of those lines, single and boundedElastic?

40
00:02:46.185 --> 00:02:48.184
The one nearest the source wins.

41
00:02:48.184 --> 00:02:50.745
The other only adds a trip on the way up.

42
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:53.182
Here the order form picture breaks.

43
00:02:53.182 --> 00:02:57.742
A real form with two lines that disagree would go back to the customer.

44
00:02:57.742 --> 00:03:00.826
Reactor quietly obeys the line nearest the oven.

45
00:03:00.826 --> 00:03:04.037
So write subscribeOn once, right after the source.

46
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.266
Now, back to the phone.

47
00:03:06.266 --> 00:03:10.984
stock.loavesLeft takes 100 milliseconds, and its thread does nothing else.

48
00:03:10.984 --> 00:03:16.212
Make that call on parallel, and you take one of its few quick hands off the belts.

49
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.131
So wrap it.

50
00:03:17.131 --> 00:03:21.185
fromCallable makes the call lazy, so it runs when subscribed.

51
00:03:21.185 --> 00:03:26.767
And subscribeOn(boundedElastic) sends it to the back room, where waiting is allowed.

52
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:29.235
Eight shops, eight calls.

53
00:03:29.235 --> 00:03:38.558
Here's the version that looks right: flatMap over the shops, each one a fromCallable, and one subscribeOn(boundedElastic) on the outside.

54
00:03:38.558 --> 00:03:40.055
How long does it take?

55
00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:46.550
813 milliseconds, on one thread, boundedElastic-1.

56
00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.014
The calls took turns.

57
00:03:48.014 --> 00:03:56.932
flatMap subscribes to each call on the thread it's running on, and fromCallable makes its call right there, as it is subscribed.

58
00:03:57.067 --> 00:04:02.066
We booked the whole back room, then sent one worker in with a list of eight calls.

59
00:04:02.066 --> 00:04:04.504
So put subscribeOn inside, on each call.

60
00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:10.477
Now every subscription goes to its own worker: 116 milliseconds, on eight threads.

61
00:04:10.477 --> 00:04:13.523
Same eight calls, about seven times faster.

62
00:04:13.523 --> 00:04:20.751
And cap them with flatMap's concurrency, so a stock book that takes 20 connections isn't asked for 40.

63
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:27.363
So a blocking call needs fromCallable, and subscribeOn(boundedElastic), on its own Mono.

64
00:04:27.363 --> 00:04:32.597
And never wait with block inside a chain on parallel: Reactor refuses it there.

65
00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:39.887
Here it is in Java and Kotlin: one wrapped call, and the shops in order, at most 4 calls at a time.

66
00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.265
So, which one moves what?

67
00:04:42.265 --> 00:04:46.932
publishOn is a hand-over point on the belt: it moves everything after it.

68
00:04:46.932 --> 00:04:52.943
subscribeOn is the line on the order form: it says who starts the oven, wherever you write it.

69
00:04:52.943 --> 00:04:56.907
And a phone call goes to the back room, one wrapped call each.

70
00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:57.893
Quick check.

71
00:04:57.893 --> 00:05:04.439
subscribeOn(single) right after the source, and publishOn(parallel) just before the subscriber.

72
00:05:04.439 --> 00:05:06.644
Where does a map in between run?

73
00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:10.422
On single-1.

74
00:05:10.422 --> 00:05:14.890
The source started there, and the map comes before the hand-over point.

75
00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.281
Only the subscriber moves to parallel.
